.TH INVF-VERIFY 8 "2026-09" "InvariantFS" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
invf-verify \- verify an InvariantFS volume's integrity
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B invf-verify
.IR image | device
.RB [ \-\-deep | \-\-verify\-seal | \-\-list\-damaged ]
.RB [ \-\-ignore\-missing\-codecs ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B invf-verify
checks an InvariantFS volume for structural and content integrity. The
volume is opened read-only and is
.I not
locked: a mounted InvariantFS can be verified while it runs.
.PP
The default pass validates the superblock (magic, checksum, block size),
the block bitmap accounting, and the Meta-v3 base B+ tree. With
.B \-\-deep
every stored byte of every file is additionally decoded through the real
read path - batches, container rebuilds, codec blobs - and checked, so a
deep verify proves the volume serves bit-exact content end to end.
.PP
The
.BR invfs-verify.timer (8)
systemd unit runs a weekly deep verify over the volumes listed in
.IR /etc/invfs/volumes .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-deep
Decode and compare every file's content, not just the structures.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-damaged
Print the per-file damage ledger: one tab-separated line per live file the
volume cannot read back fully, as
.IR "damaged<TAB>id<TAB>name<TAB>reason<TAB>size" .
This is the same ledger, from the same core function and in the same format,
that
.BR invf-fsck (8)
.B \-\-list\-damaged
prints; the two outputs are byte-identical for the same image. It reads
recipe blobs (not file content), which is the expensive half of fsck, so it is
a mode of its own and does not combine with
.B \-\-deep
or
.BR \-\-verify\-seal .
Exits 0 when the list is empty, 3 when it is not, 1 when the walk could not
complete (fail closed: a short list proves nothing).
.TP
.B \-\-verify\-seal
Read-only seal scrub: report per-group parity drift, change nothing.
Exits 0 clean, 3 on drift, 1 on tool failure. No sweep runs.
.TP
.B \-\-ignore\-missing\-codecs
Do not fail when a file's codec pack is absent; report those files as
unverifiable instead.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the volume is clean at the level that was performed; 1 on any failure
(the failures are enumerated on stderr), and on content damage found by
.BR \-\-deep ;
2 on usage errors.
.PP
The distinction matters: without
.B \-\-deep
this tool validates the superblock, the zone table and the bitmap, and reads
no file content at all, so it exits 0 on a volume that has an unreadable file.
Its verdict line says so in words. Content has to be checked with
.B \-\-deep
(exits 1, names every corrupt file) or with
.BR invf-fsck (8)
(verdict DAMAGED, exit 3, and
.B \-\-list\-damaged
names each file and its reason).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR invf-fsck (8),
.BR invf-sweep (8),
.BR invarifs (7)
